.TH LINKWISE 1 "2026-11-15" "Linkwise" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
linkwise \- operate the Linkwise modem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B linkwise
.RB [ \-\-help | \-\-version ]
.br
.B linkwise encode-frame
.I level control input output
.RI [ format ]
.br
.B linkwise transmit-frame
.I level control input
.br
.B linkwise decode-frame
.I input
.br
.B linkwise send
.I source destination input
.RI [ "command-port data-port" ]
.br
.B linkwise chat
.RI [ "\-\-pki file [\-\-encrypt | \-\-enroll]" ]
.RI [ "\-\-secure-config file" ]
.I local
.RI [ "command-port data-port" ]
.br
.B linkwise mail queue
.RI [ "\-\-pki file [\-\-encrypt]" ]
.I mailbox envelope
.br
.B linkwise mail import
.RI [ "\-\-pki file [\-\-encrypt]" ]
.I mailbox input
.br
.B linkwise mail export
.RI [ "\-\-pki file [\-\-encrypt]" ]
.I mailbox message-id
.br
.B linkwise mail smtp
.I mailbox callsign
.RI [ "port [\-\-tls cert key | \-\-starttls cert key]" ]
.br
.B linkwise mail imap
.I mailbox
.RI [ "port [\-\-tls cert key]" ]
.br
.B linkwise mail
.RI { exchange | listen }
.I mailbox local peer
.RI [ "command-port data-port" ]
.br
.B linkwise pki
.RI { check | verify-chat }
.I profile
.RI [ proof ]
.br
.B linkwise pki fingerprint
.RI { ca | cert }
.I certificate
.br
.B linkwise qsl card
.I profile to pdf
.B \-\-utc
.I timestamp
.B \-\-freq-khz
.I frequency
.B \-\-mode
.I mode
.B \-\-rst
.I report
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B linkwise qsl inspect
.I photo
.br
.B linkwise qsl import
.I profile from photo signing-sha256
.RI [ encryption-sha256 ]
.RB [ \-\-replace ]
.br
.B linkwise qsl receive
.I profile from directory signing-sha256
.RI [ encryption-sha256 ]
.br
.B linkwise secure
.RI { send | receive }
.I config file
.br
.B linkwise
.RI { analyze-wav | decode-wav }
.I input
.br
.B linkwise render-wav
.I request-json output-wav
.br
.B linkwise
.RI { audio-inputs | audio-outputs }
.br
.B linkwise receive-audio
.I seconds
.br
.B linkwise receive-pcm
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no arguments, start the MCP server on standard input and output.
An optional
.B \-\-
before command operands ends option parsing.
A path written as
.B ./-
refers to a literal file named
.BR - .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B encode-frame
Render a single BW500 DATA frame to a file or standard output.
Levels 1, 2, and 3 require exactly 9, 22, and 34 payload bytes, respectively.
Supply a control byte in decimal or hexadecimal with a
.B 0x
prefix.
Input and output accept
.B -
for standard input and output.
The default format is WAV. Specify
.B pcm-f32le
for raw little-endian IEEE float samples.
Both formats use 48 kHz, mono, 32-bit samples.
A named output file is overwritten after input validation.
.TP
.B transmit-frame
Encode the same DATA frame and play it on the selected output device.
Requires an audio-enabled build. This command generates audio without
controlling PTT. Success means the device's presentation estimate elapsed.
.TP
.B decode-frame
Read a 48 kHz mono WAV and write the payload of exactly one CRC-valid
DATA frame as binary stdout. Zero or multiple frames produce an error.
.TP
.B send
Open a P2P session through the local modem command and data ports.
Send bytes from a file or standard input and wait for the modem to
acknowledge the transmit queue and report disconnection.
The default ports are 8300 and 8301.
Both custom ports must be supplied together.
The source callsign must match the configured station.
This command consumes inbound data to keep the transport flowing.
.TP
.B chat
Open an interactive direct text client for a local Linkwise modem in
VARA HF BW500 mode. The callsign must match the station configuration.
The command and DATA ports default to 8300 and 8301.
Supply both custom ports together. Only one RF client can own the modem.
.TP
.B "Chat commands"
Use
.B "/listen on"
to receive calls,
.B "/connect CALL"
to dial, and
.B "/disconnect"
to end a call. The peer window opens on connection. Enter sends one line
of up to 1024 UTF-8 bytes. Prefix a literal leading slash with a second slash.
Messages wrap at word boundaries and reflow when the terminal is resized.
Timestamped presence notices appear in the peer and Status windows.
A PKI peer joins after verification.
Use
.B /who
to show your callsign and the current peer.
Use
.B "/window N"
or Alt+number to switch windows, and
.B /help
to list commands and key bindings. Scrollback and input history are
held in memory. Use the proof command to save a verified message.
.TP
.B "Authenticated chat"
Start both clients with
.B "\-\-pki FILE"
to use the shared X.509 station profile.
The clients check the certificate and prove possession of their keys
before exchanging signed text.
Inside the callsign brackets,
.B |V
indicates signed plaintext and
.B |E
indicates signed and encrypted chat.
.B |X
marks failed verification and
.B |F
marks an encryption or decryption failure.
The callsign, status code, and start of the message occupy fixed columns.
Add
.B \-\-encrypt
on both clients to protect the signed records with mutually authenticated
TLS 1.3 using ephemeral P-256 and AES-256-GCM.
Encrypted chat requires transport opt-in and the AWS-LC FIPS provider.
The optional
.B pqc-chat
build feature supports the
.B p256_mlkem768
profile with OpenSSL FIPS provider 3.5.9.
Both peers must select that profile to use hybrid key exchange.
During a verified signed call, enter
.B /encrypt
to request an upgrade. The other operator enters the same command to accept.
Chat holds new text as a draft until both clients confirm encryption.
The call remains encrypted until disconnect.
The
.B secure-transfer
build feature enables PKI.
.TP
.B "Certificate enrollment"
Start both stations with
.B "\-\-pki FILE \-\-enroll"
and connect as usual.
Enter
.B "/cert offer"
to send a public signing certificate, or add
.B "\-\-encrypt"
to include the public encryption certificate.
The recipient runs
.B "/cert inspect"
and compares SHA-256 fingerprints over a trusted channel before running
.B "/cert accept SIGNING [ENCRYPTION]" .
Use
.B "/cert accept replace SIGNING [ENCRYPTION]"
to replace an installed certificate.
Both stations must already trust the issuing authorities and have current CRLs.
Profiles with multiple CA keys require a per-callsign CA key fingerprint.
Enrollment writes accepted certificates to the shared station profile.
Reconnect with
.B "\-\-pki FILE"
to verify the peer and exchange text.
.TP
.B "/proof PATH"
Save the latest verified incoming message from the selected peer as a private
file. This exports the message text even if the session was encrypted.
.TP
.B "Chat file transfer"
In the connected peer window,
.B /sendfile PATH
offers a gzip file with a SHA-256 checksum.
.B /accept 1
saves to the offered basename in the current directory, and
.B /accept 1 PATH
chooses another destination. The receiver publishes a verified file
without overwriting. The file limit is 32 KiB before compression.
.B /reject 1
declines an offer,
.B /cancel 1
stops the transfer and may abort the call to discard a partial record.
.B /transfers
shows the transfer state. File transfer requires the
.B secure-transfer
build feature and the AWS-LC provider in FIPS mode. Stock VarAC file
offers advertise MD5 and are declined.
.TP
.B "Encrypted chat file transfer"
Start chat with
.B --secure-config FILE
before the local callsign in a build with the
.B secure-transfer
feature. The receiver runs
.B /receivefile --encrypt PATH
before the sender runs
.B /sendfile --encrypt PATH .
The chat uses the attached modem for a dedicated mutually authenticated
TLS session with gzip and SHA-256, then returns to chat. Both peers need
Linkwise and matching certificate configurations. Authentication failure
ends the transfer. See the chat guide for setup and transport restrictions.
.TP
.B "Chat shutdown"
.B /quit
and Ctrl-C drain pending output and disconnect before exiting.
The client aborts after 120 seconds without transmit acknowledgment progress
or after 30 seconds waiting for the modem to disconnect once the queue drains.
Queued text after link loss has unconfirmed delivery and is never resent
automatically.
.TP
.B mail queue
Queue a Winlink B2F envelope of up to 1 MiB in a private mailbox directory.
Use a local filesystem with hard-link support.
.TP
.B mail import, mail export
Import an RFC 5322/MIME file into the transmit queue with
.BR "mail import" .
Export a received message as MIME to standard output with
.BR "mail export" ,
using its B2F message ID as the final operand.
.TP
.B "Protected radio mail"
Pass
.B "\-\-pki FILE"
to mail queue, import, or export to use the shared station profile.
Protected mail supports one recipient and covers the content and attachments.
Add
.B \-\-encrypt
to encrypt on import or queue, or require encrypted input on export.
Exchange and listen forward the protected envelope through the usual queue.
.TP
.B pki check
Check the station profile, certificate roles, trust chain, and revocation.
.TP
.B pki fingerprint
Print the SHA-256 fingerprint of the CA public key with
.B ca
or of the complete leaf certificate with
.BR cert .
.TP
.B pki verify-chat
Verify a proof exported by chat with the recipient's station profile and
write the signed message as JSON.
.TP
.B qsl card
Create a one-page landscape PDF for the blank side of a USPS 5.5 by 3.5
inch stamped card. Print on matching stock at actual size (100 percent).
Use
.B "\-\-size 4x6"
for 6 by 4 inch stock. Supply an RFC 3339 QSO timestamp, frequency in kHz,
mode and RST report.
.B "\-\-grid LOCATOR"
adds a Maidenhead locator and
.B "\-\-notes TEXT"
adds up to 45 printable ASCII characters. The QR symbol carries public
signing and mail-encryption certificate chains and binds them to the
intended recipient with the sender's signature. Use
.B \-\-signing-only
to omit the mail-encryption certificate. Requires secure-transfer support.
.TP
.B qsl inspect, qsl import
Read a JPEG or PNG photograph or a text file containing the QR payload.
Inspection displays the unverified offer and certificate fingerprints.
Import checks the signed sender and recipient, callsign and certificate
roles, the configured CA pin and revocation status, and the complete
SHA-256 leaf fingerprints obtained through an independent trusted channel.
Import saves the public certificates to the shared chat and mail PKI
profile. Supply the signing fingerprint first and the encryption fingerprint
when included. Use
.B \-\-replace
to authorize a changed leaf after verifying the new fingerprints.
.TP
.B qsl receive
Wait for a new or changed JPEG, PNG, or QR text file in a directory
where a phone shares its photos, then import one verified offer.
The existing peer's leaf certificates are preserved if the card offers a
replacement. Use Ctrl-C to stop waiting. A phone can share through AirDrop
or a synced folder. Export as JPEG or PNG if the original is HEIC.
.TP
.B mail smtp
Accept local SMTP submissions and queue them for B2F peer-to-peer forwarding.
The listener defaults to IPv4 loopback on port 2525.
Set
.B LINKWISE_SMTP_BIND
to override the bind address.
With TLS support in the build, append
.B PORT --tls CERT.pem KEY.pem
for implicit TLS or
.B PORT --starttls CERT.pem KEY.pem
for SMTP STARTTLS.
Set both
.B LINKWISE_SMTP_USER
and
.B LINKWISE_SMTP_PASSWORD
to require SMTP authentication over TLS.
Non-loopback listeners require TLS and authentication.
The authenticated account can submit only as the configured station.
.TP
.B mail imap
Expose the Inbox and the Sent archive over IMAP.
Clients can set or clear the persistent read flag with
.B STORE
or
.BR "UID STORE" .
EXAMINE opens either mailbox in read-only mode.
The listener defaults to IPv4 loopback on port 1143.
Set
.B LINKWISE_IMAP_BIND
to override the bind address.
With TLS support in the build, append
.B PORT --tls CERT.pem KEY.pem
to use implicit TLS.
Local connections are automatically authenticated by default.
Set both
.B LINKWISE_IMAP_USER
and
.B LINKWISE_IMAP_PASSWORD
to require IMAP LOGIN over TLS.
Non-loopback listeners require TLS and authentication.
Select the inbox or poll with NOOP to discover newly received mail.
Inbox UIDs persist across listener restarts.
Keep the
.B imap-inbox
metadata directory with the mailbox when making a backup.
IMAP clients can fetch complete messages or individual MIME sections.
Sent copies saved with APPEND retain their UIDs across restarts.
The Sent archive is capped at 64 MiB and is stored under
.B imap-sent
in the mailbox directory.
Saving a Sent copy has no effect on the transmit queue.
.TP
.B mail exchange, mail listen
Call a peer or listen for a specified peer through the local modem.
Exchange queued and incoming B2F messages in one session.
The default loopback command and data ports are 8300 and 8301.
Supply both port overrides together.
Run one exchange at a time per mailbox.
Incoming envelopes are stored under
.B inbox
using the lowercase hexadecimal encoding of the message ID as the filename,
with a
.B .b2f
suffix.
Success requires B2F termination and a modem disconnection notice.
.TP
.B secure send, secure receive
Transfer one payload between two Linkwise peers using mutual TLS 1.3
with 256-bit AES-GCM traffic keys.
Requires a source build with the
.B secure-transfer
feature. Standard modem installers omit this feature.
Both peers require a separate JSON configuration with
.B encryption.enabled
set to
.BR true .
The default is disabled. The configuration specifies local and peer
callsigns, loopback command and data ports, certificates, and a size limit.
The receiver checks the byte count and SHA-256 digest before publishing
a named file and refuses to overwrite an existing file.
The sender checks the authenticated receipt.
Both commands wait for the modem to report disconnection
before returning success.
See the encrypted P2P transfer guide in the source tree
.RI (rust/docs/secure-transfer.md)
for the configuration format and certificate setup.
.TP
.B analyze-wav
Report burst candidates as a JSON object.
.TP
.B decode-wav
Report burst candidates and CRC-valid DATA frames as a JSON object.
Each frame includes its level, sample range, payload in hexadecimal,
and control byte. Both WAV commands accept
.B -
for stdin and support 16-bit PCM and 32-bit float mono WAV input.
Decoding requires 48 kHz.
.TP
.B render-wav
Render a BW500 handshake stimulus from a JSON file to a WAV file.
Write a JSON summary to stdout.
.TP
.B audio-inputs, audio-outputs
List native audio device names as JSON arrays.
.TP
.B receive-audio
Receive for 1 to 3600 seconds. Write one JSON object per burst candidate,
with an array of decoded frames. Each frame includes its control byte.
.TP
.B receive-pcm
Read continuous mono 48 kHz signed little-endian 16-bit PCM on standard input
and stream BW500 receive events as JSON lines.
Feed the raw sample stream without a file header.
Each process accepts one continuous stream.
.SH STANDARD STREAMS
Frame payloads and waveforms use binary streams. Reports use JSON.
Diagnostics go to standard error.
For a frame input, EOF ends the payload and an empty, short, or oversized
payload fails before encoding or playback.
For
.BR send ,
EOF ends the transfer. Empty input succeeds without opening a session.
For
.BR "secure send" ,
a file operand of
.B -
reads standard input. For
.BR "secure receive" ,
it writes binary standard output, which may contain partial data if the
transfer fails.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LINKWISE_AUDIO_INPUT
Exact input device name. Defaults to the system input.
.TP
.B LINKWISE_AUDIO_CHANNEL
Zero-based input channel. Defaults to 0.
.TP
.B LINKWISE_AUDIO_OUTPUT
Exact output device name. Required for
.BR transmit-frame .
.TP
.B LINKWISE_AUDIO_OUTPUT_CHANNEL
Zero-based output channel. Defaults to 0.
.TP
.B LINKWISE_BW500_PROFILE
Optional level 4 measurement profile for decoding. Required for rendering
a BW500 setup stimulus.
.TP
.B LINKWISE_RADIO_BACKEND
MCP radio backend. Defaults to
.BR simulated .
Set to
.B rigctld
to use Hamlib.
.TP
.B LINKWISE_RIGCTLD_ADDR
MCP rigctld endpoint. Defaults to
.BR hamlib:4532 .
.TP
.B LINKWISE_RADIO_WRITES
Enable MCP radio writes with
.BR true .
Requires station limits.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The selected operation completed.
.TP
.B 1
The operation failed, including I/O errors and a closed output pipe.
.TP
.B 2
The command or its operands were invalid, or the build lacks
secure transfer support.
.TP
.B 130
A live send, transmit, or receive command was interrupted by SIGINT
or by Ctrl-C or Ctrl-Break on Windows.
.TP
.B 143
A live command was interrupted by SIGTERM on Unix.
.SH SIGNALS
An interrupted send closes both modem sockets.
Delivery of bytes still awaiting acknowledgment is uncertain.
For a secure transfer, the receiver may already have published the file
if the session ends before the sender obtains the receipt.
Interrupting live audio releases the device.
A closed stdout pipe causes a quiet output failure.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Print a QSL card with public certificates
.EX
linkwise qsl card station-pki.json BB2BB qsl.pdf \\
  \-\-utc 2026-11-15T19:30:00Z \-\-freq-khz 7105 \-\-mode BW500 \-\-rst 59
.EE
.SS Receive a QSL card from a phone photo
Start the receiver, then share a close JPEG or PNG photo of the QR symbol
to the watched directory. Replace the fingerprint placeholders with the
complete SHA-256 fingerprints obtained from the station.
.EX
linkwise qsl receive station-pki.json AA1AA "$HOME/Downloads" \\
  SIGNING_SHA256 ENCRYPTION_SHA256
.EE
.SS Attach terminal chat to a configured BW500 station
Start the station daemon, then open a second terminal using the callsign
from its configuration.
.EX
linkwise-modemd --serve-station station.json
linkwise chat YOURCALL
.EE
In the chat terminal, use
.B "/connect PEERCALL"
or
.B "/listen on" .
.SS Round trip without a radio
Create a nine-byte payload that includes a NUL byte. Write a WAV and
verify that decoding recovers the exact input.
.EX
printf '\e00012345678' >payload.bin
linkwise encode-frame 1 0xa5 payload.bin tx.wav
linkwise decode-frame tx.wav >received.bin
cmp payload.bin received.bin
.EE
.SS Pipe the waveform and redirect diagnostics
.EX
linkwise encode-frame 1 0xa5 - - <payload.bin 2>encode.log |
    linkwise decode-frame - >received.bin 2>decode.log
cmp payload.bin received.bin
.EE
.SS Inspect the decoded control byte
.EX
linkwise decode-wav tx.wav | jq '.bw500_data_frames[].control'
.EE
.SS Play a frame through an isolated audio device
Use the exact name reported by
.BR audio-outputs .
.EX
linkwise audio-outputs
LINKWISE_AUDIO_OUTPUT='Loopback Device' \\
    linkwise transmit-frame 1 0xa5 - <payload.bin
.EE
.SS Send a file through the running modem
Replace the callsigns with your configured source and intended peer.
.EX
linkwise send W1AW K1ABC message.bin 2>send.log
.EE
.SS Exchange queued Winlink mail
.nf
linkwise mail queue ./mailbox message.b2f
linkwise mail exchange ./mailbox N0CALL W1AW
.fi
.SS Import and export MIME mail
Import an email for the next peer exchange.
Export a received message by its B2F message ID.
.EX
linkwise mail import ./mailbox outgoing.eml
linkwise mail export ./mailbox TEST123 >received.eml
.EE
.SS Send an encrypted file
Using secure-enabled builds, start the first command on the receiving
station and the second on the sending station. The two JSON files
contain the respective station's secure transfer settings.
.EX
linkwise secure receive receiver.json received.bin
linkwise secure send sender.json original.bin 2>secure-send.log
.EE
.SS Read an encrypted transfer payload from standard input
With the receiver listening, use shell input redirection to send a file.
.EX
linkwise secure send sender.json - <original.bin
.EE
.SS Authenticate a station and verify a chat message
.EX
linkwise pki check station-pki.json
linkwise chat --pki station-pki.json AA1AA
linkwise pki verify-chat station-pki.json message-proof.json
.EE
.SS Queue signed radio email
.EX
linkwise mail import --pki station-pki.json ./mailbox outgoing.eml
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR linkwise-modemd (8),
.BR linkwise-station (5)
